<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Noah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nōaḥ, Noach, Nūḥ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Noah appears as the last of the Antediluvian patriarchs in the traditions of the Abrahamic religions, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a> (chapters 5–9), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> and Baha'i writings. In the Genesis flood narrative God decides to destroy the world by flood because of humanity's evil, and Noah builds the Ark, saving his family and the land animals; afterwards God makes a covenant with him, marked by the rainbow, never again to flood the earth. Genesis presents him as the first to cultivate the vine and says he died at the age of 950. Most scholars hold that the account drew on earlier Mesopotamian flood traditions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/noah.jpg" alt="Noah" width="330" height="549" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noah_icon_17c..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s name survives in the biblical Ararat, where in the Book of Genesis <b>Noah</b>’s ark came to rest; the modern Mount Ararat received that name much later.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Muslims believe that God sent prophets to every people, among them Adam, <b>Noah</b>, Abraham, Moses and Jesus, and that Muhammad is the last of them, the “seal of the prophets”.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Its literature preserved the earliest versions of stories that travelled far: the adventures of Gilgamesh and a great flood, whose survivor parallels Utnapishtim of the Akkadian epic and <b>Noah</b> of the Bible.</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward Chiera, Hermann Volrath Hilprecht, Samuel <b>Noah</b> Kramer and others published further copies, from Nippur and from the Istanbul, Paris, London and Baltimore collections, and more came from excavations at Isin, Kish, Susa and Ur.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kesh-temple-hymn">Kesh Temple Hymn</a></li><li><blockquote>Samuel <b>Noah</b> Kramer joined twenty-two fragments into the first complete edition, published by the University of Chicago in 1940.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Noah</b> Webster's American Dictionary of the English Language, with some 70,000 entries, was published.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-14">14 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
